{
  "title": "LeanDojo: Theorem Proving with Retrieval-Augmented Language Models",
  "authors": [
    "Kaiyu Yang",
    "Aidan M. Swope",
    "Alex Gu",
    "Rahul Chalamala",
    "Peiyang Song",
    "Shixing Yu",
    "Saad Godil",
    "Ryan Prenger",
    "Anima Anandkumar"
  ],
  "year": 2023,
  "arxivId": "2306.15626",
  "abstract": "LeanDojo is an open-source toolkit that enables the extraction of data from the Lean proof assistant and interaction with it programmatically. This paper introduces ReProver, a retrieval-augmented language model for theorem proving that leverages LeanDojo's capabilities. ReProver achieves state-of-the-art results on the LeanDojo Benchmark, outperforming non-retrieval baselines and GPT-4.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Theorem proving is a challenging task that involves generating proofs for theorems expressed in formal logic. <strong>Retrieval-augmented language models</strong> can be used to improve the efficiency of theorem proving by selecting relevant premises from a large math library. LeanDojo is a toolkit that provides a platform for extracting data from the Lean proof assistant and interacting with it programmatically.",
